section('equipment','Native service housings, probes and finish (0.22.0)', '''`EquipmentServices` owns the physical enclosure, mounting brackets, DATA/FE datums and solids. `ServiceSurface` queries native facets; it does not substitute an equipment bounding box for a support. The plan is imported by `ShapeRasterizer` before placement/collision tables are made. Rendering, constructor ghosts, world registration and the drawing exporter read that same plan. Coarse meshes retain Normal case/socket datums and reproject bracket contacts onto their own facets.

Horizontal drums use a saddle side; spheres use a clamped service leg; floating tanks use the front gauge/service area. Heater mounts avoid native ladder clearance. Pump/compressor cases fit the drive-end deck. Rotating DATA sides follow shaft power flow, with FE opposite; large motor/generator cases are matched. The turbine reuses its existing governor enclosure. Inline movers have integrated terminal necks and the control valve a side-facing positioner. `SERVICE_CASE` and `SERVICE_COVER` use equipment BODY paint; connector identity rings and instrument steel keep their own finishes.

`EquipmentInstruments` is the single probe-location/manual-policy catalog. `InstrumentGeometry` adapts it for mounts and supplies shared transmitter poses/preview heads. `InstrumentWiring` supplies reinforced roots, short 20 mm protected metallic drops and long surface-textured runs. `SurfaceSection`/`SurfaceJoints` follow actual facets and connected supports. Branches share an orthogonal baseline; curved-facet strips retain full width and a continuous texture station. These finishes add no process nodes or physical collision. Long-run geometry is built lazily for rendering, not by server process ticks.

Constructor previews include factory caps and level heads. Manual instruments remain accessory facts and appear when fitted; drawings explicitly show those mount options fitted for review. The native book/exporter is `tools/equipment-drawings/`; historical Python proposals are labelled separately and are not overlaid on implemented geometry. The 96-entry layout corpus records 14 cell/stamp changes from this native-interface addition, with equipment boxes, process-nozzle cells and shaft terminals retained.''')
docs['equipment']=docs['equipment'].replace("`InstrumentGeometry.levelSockets()` gives separate body/boot pads on the rear shell; `sockets()` adds horizontal-drum P/T pads. `transmitter()` is a cached six-face blank enclosure, with no needle, numbers or world display. Interaction lookup uses each loaded shape's actual bounds, including the rear pads on large vessels.","`EquipmentInstruments` owns vessel probe placement; the `InstrumentGeometry` socket APIs are adapters. Body and boot level IDs remain distinct. Upright closed transmitter housings and bounds-aware interaction use the same native pose, including top-platform instruments on spheres.")
docs['equipment']=docs['equipment'].replace('`DataSocketGeometry.drum` gives standing/lying drums separate shell terminal pads;','`DataSocketGeometry` reads the shared service housing sockets;')
section('instrument-accessories','Manual mounts and factory instruments (0.22.0)', '''`EquipmentInstruments` supplies physical probe locations and eligibility. Standing drums have P/T/body-level taps on a front roof row, reserving rear F1/F2 footprints for future blanketing/PSV fittings. Horizontal drums have crown P/T/body-level taps and a separate boot level tap. Sphere P/T/level heads sit on supported top-platform mounts. Floating tanks retain grade-level P/T and their digital level mount beside the front service area; the mechanical liquid-level gauge remains optional.

Rotating equipment, columns, exchangers, kettles, air coolers and fired heaters accept no new manual equipment probes. Their accepted internal channels are in `BuiltInInputs`. Both cooling towers own inlet/basin T probes and a built-in basin level head, at the retained level location. Level means liquid plus retained solid volume, excluding gas. Ordinary pipe/control-valve accessory rules remain separate.

`InstrumentHosts.equipmentMount` and `Sensor.levelMounts` adapt this catalog for selection, reach, occupancy and bounds. Digital heads and analog gauges reuse `InstrumentGeometry`; protected process roots and surface leads come from `InstrumentWiring`. The renderer uses installed mount IDs, actual exchanger nozzle sizes and the selected detail profile; it never requests or computes process state. Removing a pipe's last sensor retires its socket through `ControlService.bind`, preserving the cable as an open end.

Installed instruments retain accessory UUIDs and human IDs. Factory channel UUIDs derive from physical equipment identity plus channel name, with no accessory allocation; locations are hover information and never identity. Missing/unsupported quantities are unavailable, not zero. Old installed facts are retained or recovered by the existing accessory lifecycle, rather than replaced by factory allocations.''')
s=docs['instrument-accessories'];s=s.replace('Horizontal drums have shared P/T sockets and separate rear body/boot level pads.','Horizontal drums have distinct top P/T sockets and separate body/boot level identities.');s=s.replace('`InstrumentService` queues sensor installation/removal to its 100-online-tick transaction deadline,','`InstrumentService` installs/removes structural sensors immediately on interaction,');s=s.replace('| Digital level sensor | Drum, floating-roof tank, sphere or cooling basin |','| Digital level sensor | Drum, floating-roof tank or sphere; cooling-basin level is factory fitted |');s=s.replace('| Speed sensor | Steam turbine output coupling |','| Speed sensor | No manual rotating-equipment mount; use the factory shaft-speed channel |');docs['instrument-accessories']=s
section('wired-control-power','Physical interfaces and accepted channel catalog (0.22.0)', '''`EquipmentServices` owns supported enclosure solids and physical socket datums; `DataSocketGeometry`, `DataPorts` and `PowerPorts` adapt that plan. Inline movers and the pneumatic control-valve positioner expose integrated side entries. One physical C1 represents a multi-registration heat unit; secondary fluid registrations alias to its primary host. Columns reserve a control identity in the world's existing namespace and reuse it on reload. Saved wired face points remain facts.

`ControlInput` represents either an installed sensor UUID or a deterministic equipment/channel UUID. `BuiltInInputs.Binding` maps the physical host, shape/origin and fluid registrations. Movers expose inlet/outlet P/T, flow and speed; motors/generators expose speed. Heat units expose accepted process/utility port P/T and exchanger flow channels. Cooling towers expose inlet/outlet P/T and liquid-plus-solid basin level. Columns expose top/bottom P/T, reflux P/T/flow and native/external stream channels; cached assembly members add their accepted port/utility channels through C1. Unsupported air properties and V3's unmodelled circulating pumparound streams remain unavailable.

`ControlService` refreshes the cached assembly-member catalog before validating edits. It reads only accepted `FluidView`/pipe observations and column publications, for requested or enabled-loop hosts, on the 100-tick cadence. The column stores a bounded `ColumnUnit.Probes` state snapshot alongside its table; internal mass flows scale with accepted total feed, including feed water. Reload preserves these readings without a GUI-triggered solve; older rebuildable state gets its normal scheduled refresh.

The DCS is a two-block console (`DcsBlock`, `DcsGeometry`), one lower-half panel identity/menu/socket. Placement and removal treat both halves together; a legacy one-block console waits for room without moving saved identity or endpoints. Protocols: controller `control-3`, shared commands `lines-1`, tool settings `pipeline-tool-5`. Screens preserve the player's GUI scale and scroll/page within their viewport. Hover shows sensor block coordinates and attached EQ identity, with no dimension identifier.''')
s=docs['wired-control-power'];s=re.sub(r'`ConduitLaying`','`ConduitLineBackend`',s);s=s.replace('Protocol `control-2`','Protocol `control-3`').replace('protocol `control-2`','protocol `control-3`');s=s.replace('`ControlService`, `ControlSettings` |','`ControlService`, `ControlSettings`, `ControlInput`, `BuiltInInputs` |');docs['wired-control-power']=s
section('column-operation','Accepted instrumentation (0.22.0)', '''`ColumnSolve.Solved` carries the already accepted `V3ColumnInspection` to the scheduled application boundary. `ColumnOperation` reduces it to `ColumnUnit.Probes`: top/bottom/reflux pressure and temperature, and internal mass-per-total-feed coefficients. The denominator is `reference.alpha * reference.setpoints.designFeedRate`, including feed water; V3's hydrocarbon-only vector is not that basis. The latest accepted cycle supplies actual throughput. Stream boundary readings use existing accepted cycle accounts and conditions.

`FluidFacts.columnState` round-trips this bounded snapshot as rebuildable state, not player facts. Restoring it runs no solver. A state without the snapshot can keep its split table while the normal engine deadline asks for a refresh. `FluidWorldAuthority.columnInstrument` is read-only; opening a controller never advances a column. The duty/energy/heat-in-transit contract is unchanged. Pumparound specifications currently prescribe heat only, so no fictitious circulation flow or return temperature is created. `ColumnProbeTest` covers the wet-feed mass basis; `ColumnRunTest` covers saved-reading reload and continuing operation.''')
section('fluid-runtime','Service/control publication additions (0.22.0)', '''Physical DATA/FE bindings use the shared `EquipmentServices` plan. Multi-side equipment has a primary control host and aliases for its fluid registrations; a column reserves a structural control identity without a process edit. `instrumentMoverPort`, `instrumentVesselPorts`, `instrumentAt` and `columnInstrument` reduce accepted presentations for factory channels. Mover discharge pressure uses the accepted actuator outlet observation; heat-port phase volumes are rates on the same mass-flow basis, not vessel inventories. Reads never solve or advance time.

Accepted column probe coefficients/readings join only the topology state section (`ColumnUnit.Probes`, `FluidFacts.columnState`); fluid facts schema and player quantities are unchanged by this addition. Structural line edits now enter through `LineLaying`/`LineLayout`, then `FluidLineBackend` for one queued fluid registration edit or `ConduitLineBackend` for cable facts. Derived `LinePieces` copies supply cross-cell lead collision/picking; they are not new fluid devices.''')
section('cooling-towers','Factory instrument locations (0.22.0)', '''Both drafts now own inlet/basin temperature probes and a built-in basin level head at the retained gauge location. They have no new player-installed equipment sensor mounts. `EquipmentInstruments` provides their datums and `InstrumentWiring` the reinforced roots, protected drops and surface finish to the supported service enclosure. Factory level includes liquids plus solids and excludes gas. `BuiltInInputs` exposes accepted water inlet/outlet P/T and basin level through C1; no fresh Merkel/property solve occurs when a controller opens. Preview, placed heads and native drawings share these locations.''')
section('heat-exchangers','Factory probes and one physical data interface (0.22.0)', '''Both exchanger types have four equipment-owned temperature probe roots on the actual nozzle necks. `EquipmentInstruments` rebuilds these datums from live nozzle size codes, and `InstrumentWiring` follows the real nozzle/body facets. The mounted service enclosure and one C1 are shared by rendering, physical layout and registration; each heat-side fluid ID aliases to that physical host for controller visibility. Accepted P/T/flow readings remain per named process/utility role. Factory probes appear in constructor previews; manual exchanger sensor placement is disabled. This adds no thermal routing, pressure-law or column duty change.''')
section('rotating-machinery','Service side, factory probes and RPM controls (0.22.0)', '''Constructed rotating equipment uses internal inlet/outlet P/T, flow and shaft-speed channels rather than manual sensor mounts. `EquipmentServices.serviceSide` derives the DATA side from shaft power flow, so connected driver/driven machines present it on the same service side; FE is opposite. Small pump/compressor cases fit the drive-end deck, large motor/generator enclosures are matched, and turbine sockets reuse its governor housing. Case/cover finishes follow equipment body paint.

Constructor controls expose rated/design RPM and no longer offer a 2/4-pole selector. Internal/saved pole fields are not a user-facing design choice. Motors remain frequency-controlled with the 3000 rpm ceiling. The governor and accepted shaft/runtime power laws are unchanged by the interface work; instrumentation is read-only. `VisualRotation` remains the common nonlinear visual-speed law, independent of process RPM.''')
section('process-simulator','Current viewport and constructor handoff (0.22.0)', '''The screen retains the player's GUI scale; `WindowScale` is removed. Layout and scrolling use the actual GUI viewport. Equipment type/count/dimensions remain separate from Auto/Manual sizing method, with constructor-style Manual controls and build requirements emphasized. Constructor nozzle controls now join its main door; no separate Nozzles navigation is required. Pump/generator constructors display rated/design RPM, not a pole selector. Sizing facts and dormant saved dimensions remain facts; these GUI changes do not alter the V3 solve or Send-to-block duty contract.''')
section('process-control','Manual and equipment-owned signal sources (0.22.0)', '''The current placement catalog is `EquipmentInstruments`: vessel roof/top-platform mounts are manual; rotating, column, thermal and tower instruments are built in. `ControlInput`/`BuiltInInputs` add stable equipment/channel identities without allocating accessory facts. Physical instruments retain their saved UUIDs. Level measurements include liquid plus solid volume, excluding gas. Controller/DCS choices are limited to equipment connected to the selected controller (including column assembly members through its C1), with location/EQ ID on hover. See [instrument-accessories.md](instrument-accessories.md) and [wired-control-power.md](wired-control-power.md) for the actual sources and availability limits.''')
section('machine-assemblies','Column C1 instrumentation (0.22.0)', '''`ControlService.refreshColumnMembers` reads the accepted cached `AssemblyReport` and loaded member registration identities. It does not walk or validate pipes from a GUI request, load chunks, or run the column solver. Stable member/slot/channel identities expose accepted member process and utility readings through the column's physical C1. Existing assembly/column ownership still reserves its outputs; monitoring does not replace its duty/energy-transfer controller. Line placement/edit notifications now come through the shared `LineLaying` owners.''')
